{
  "runId": "sbps-radar-improvements-ms4tjeiu-o15c48",
  "workflowName": "sbps-radar-improvements",
  "script": "export const meta = {\n  name: \"sbps-radar-improvements\",\n  description: \"Audit SBPS radar codebase and research external data feeds; synthesize prioritized improvement opportunities into a markdown report\",\n  phases: [{ title: \"Investigate\" }, { title: \"Synthesize\" }],\n};\n\n// No schema — agent() returns plain text. We parse JSON in-JS with guards.\nfunction extractItems(text) {\n  if (!text) return [];\n  let s = String(text).trim();\n  // Strip markdown code fences if present.\n  s = s.replace(/^```(?:json)?\\s*/i, \"\").replace(/\\s*```$/i, \"\");\n  // Find the first JSON object/array bounds.\n  const start = s.search(/[[{]/);\n  if (start < 0) return [];\n  s = s.slice(start);\n  try {\n    const parsed = JSON.parse(s);\n    if (Array.isArray(parsed)) return parsed;\n    if (parsed && Array.isArray(parsed.items)) return parsed.items;\n    return [];\n  } catch {\n    // Greedy: keep only up to last balanced close — naive fallback.\n    const lastObj = s.lastIndexOf(\"}\");\n    const lastArr = s.lastIndexOf(\"]\");\n    const end = Math.max(lastObj, lastArr);\n    if (end > 0) {\n      try {\n        const parsed = JSON.parse(s.slice(0, end + 1));\n        if (Array.isArray(parsed)) return parsed;\n        if (parsed && Array.isArray(parsed.items)) return parsed.items;\n      } catch {}\n    }\n    return [];\n  }\n}\n\nconst bizCtx = `PROJECT: SBPS Supply Radar — AI signal monitor for Single Barrel Power Solutions, a turnkey broker in industrial power generation equipment.\nCORE THESIS: Equipment availability IS the product (supply-starved market). The alpha is TIME-TO-SIGNAL — finding surplus/available equipment before broker chains form and mark up prices. SBPS is sole intermediary: title holder <-> SBPS <-> buyer, transparent 1/1/1 commission.\nMODEL: title holder <-> SBPS <-> buyer. Global scope (50/60Hz, voltage, grid code, logistics are hard match gates). Title-holder verification is non-negotiable (distinguish real owners from brokers claiming to represent sellers).\nPIPELINE: Collect -> Classify (LLM) -> Enrich -> Cross-reference contacts -> Score -> Call sheet. SQLite store with dedup.\nCURRENT SOURCES: EIA-860 (plant retirements/generator status), SEC EDGAR 8-K (mergers/bankruptcies/asset dispositions), FERC eLibrary (project withdrawals/permit cancellations), PACER (bankruptcy filings with listed equipment assets).\nKNOWN STATUS (from handoff): EIA collector yields 3892 raw signals -> 521 after dedup -> 39 unique companies. SEC 8-K via Atom feed works. Buyer matching: 114 buyers loaded, 514/521 signals match >=1 buyer. Contact matching: 39 seller companies ALL blind spots (none in buyer list). Call sheet ranked by alpha. SQLite dedup + actionable_contacts.csv export. 32 tests passing, lint clean.\nPHASE PLAN: Phase 1 (now) = public signal monitoring + call sheet. Phase 2 = cert partner intake, title verification, frequency/voltage matching, blind-spot discovery, persistence hardening. Phase 3 = auction house foundation, insurance partnerships, broker registry.\nNAMED HIGH-ALPHA FUTURE SOURCES: certification partners (inspect equipment before any sale -> see distress FIRST; highest alpha but needs relationship setup), auction houses, insurance partners, broker registry. Also mentioned: behind-the-meter/data-center self-gen, turbine blade failures -> repair quotes -> sell decision, conference attendee lists as discovery, new surplus sitting in warehouses (cancelled projects, bankrupt developers).`;\n\nphase(\"Investigate\");\nconst [auditText, feedsText] = await parallel([\n  () => agent(\n    `You are auditing the SBPS Supply Radar Python codebase to find concrete IMPROVEMENT opportunities.\\n\\n${bizCtx}\\n\\nYOUR TASK: Read the actual source under src/Sbps/ (files: cli.py, models.py, store.py, config.py, classifier.py, enricher.py, matcher.py, scorer.py, verifier.py, call_sheet.py, buyer_matcher.py, cert_intake.py, llm/client.py, collectors/base.py, collectors/eia.py, collectors/sec.py, collectors/ferc.py, collectors/pacer.py). Read the ones relevant to each finding.\\n\\nIdentify concrete improvement opportunities. Look for: collectors specced but not fully built (FERC, PACER), weak/naive scoring, missing or shallow enrichment, LLM classifier quality/prompt issues, missing error handling/retries/rate-limiting, pagination/dedup gaps, no scheduling/automation, title verification not implemented, frequency/voltage matching gaps, blind-spot discovery logic quality, buyer-matcher match-gate rigor, observability/logging, test coverage holes, config/secrets handling, data freshness. Also flag quick wins vs deeper work.\\n\\nFor EACH opportunity produce one JSON object with fields: title, kind (always \"internal\"), description, alphaRationale, feasibility, effort (one of S/M/L), phase (one of 1/2/3), priority (number 1-5, 1=highest), evidence (cite real file paths/functions you read), source (empty string). Aim for 8-14 grounded items.\\n\\nReturn ONLY a JSON array of these objects. No prose, no markdown fences, no commentary — just the raw JSON array starting with [ and ending with ].`,\n    { label: \"codebase-audit\", tier: \"medium\" },\n  ),\n  () => agent(\n    `You are researching EXTERNAL data feeds / signal sources that could enhance the SBPS Supply Radar's time-to-signal alpha beyond the 4 current sources (EIA-860, SEC 8-K, FERC, PACER).\\n\\n${bizCtx}\\n\\nYOUR TASK: Research and evaluate candidate external signal sources for surplus/available industrial power equipment (gas/steam turbines, generators, transformers, switchgear, etc.). Use PRIMARY sources (official sites, APIs, docs) and cite URLs. Cover:\\n- The NAMED Phase 2/3 sources: certification/inspection partners, auction houses for industrial/power equipment, insurance partners (recovery/retirement signals), broker registries.\\n- NEWLY discovered high-alpha sources, e.g.: industry auction/marketplace platforms, OEM remarketing/used-equipment portals, decommissioning/project-cancellation databases, data-center capacity market signals, grid/operator retirement announcements (ISO/RTO interconnection queues, generator deactivation notices), EPA/environmental retirement & compliance signals, shipping/port/customs import-export signals, insurance/loss registers, conference/exhibition attendee/exhibitor lists, trade-press distress feeds, LinkedIn/company signals, international equivalents (50Hz markets — Europe/Asia/Africa grid operators, ENTSO-E, etc.).\\n\\nFor EACH source produce one JSON object with fields: title, kind (always \"feed\"), description, alphaRationale, feasibility (free vs paid + access method), effort (one of S/M/L), phase (one of 1/2/3), priority (number 1-5), evidence (short), source (real URL). Prioritize sources that see availability BEFORE broker chains form. Aim for 10-16 grounded items.\\n\\nReturn ONLY a JSON array of these objects. No prose, no markdown fences, no commentary — just the raw JSON array starting with [ and ending with ].`,\n    { label: \"data-feed-research\", tier: \"medium\" },\n  ),\n]);\n\nconst auditItems = extractItems(auditText);\nconst feedItems = extractItems(feedsText);\nlog(`audit items: ${auditItems.length} (raw null? ${auditText === null}), feed items: ${feedItems.length} (raw null? ${feedsText === null})`);\n\nphase(\"Synthesize\");\nconst combined = auditItems.concat(feedItems);\nconst doc = await agent(\n  `You are writing the final deliverable for the SBPS client: a prioritized improvement-opportunities report for their supply radar. Below is a JSON array of candidate items (internal codebase improvements kind=\"internal\", and external data feeds kind=\"feed\"), each with title, description, alphaRationale, feasibility, effort, phase, priority, evidence, source.\\n\\n${JSON.stringify(combined)}\\n\\nWrite ONE complete Markdown document. Requirements:\\n- Title: \"SBPS Supply Radar — Improvement Opportunities\".\\n- Short intro (3-4 sentences) framing the goal: raise time-to-signal alpha and deal quality.\\n- \"Prioritization method\" line: alpha (time-to-signal / deal-quality impact) x feasibility (access + effort).\\n- Section A \"Internal Pipeline Improvements\": a markdown table with columns Title | Alpha | Feasibility | Effort | Phase | Evidence(file). Then a \"Top internal picks\" bullet list (3-5) with one-line rationale each.\\n- Section B \"External Data Feeds\": a markdown table with columns Source | Signal | Alpha | Feasibility(free/paid + access) | Effort | Phase | URL. Then a \"Top feed picks\" bullet list (3-5) with one-line rationale.\\n- Section \"Top 5 — do next\": a ranked combined list across both sections, each with a 1-2 sentence why-now.\\n- Section \"Phase roadmap\": 3-4 bullets mapping work to Phase 1/2/3.\\n- Be concrete and specific; cite the evidence/URLs from the items. No generic filler, no hype. If an item is weak, drop it rather than pad.\\n- Output the Markdown document ONLY, no wrapping commentary.`,\n  { label: \"synthesize-report\", tier: \"big\" },\n);\n\nreturn {\n  internalCount: auditItems.length,\n  feedCount: feedItems.length,\n  auditFailed: auditText === null,\n  feedsFailed: feedsText === null,\n  doc,\n};",
